+# BLOCKED: exact median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to return the **exact** median of a
+single-pass generator of arbitrary floats while using less than 64 KB of heap:
+
+```python
+exact = sorted(feed())
+want = (exact[99_999] + exact[100_000]) / 2
+del exact
+tracemalloc.start()
+got = median(feed())
+_, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory()
+...
+self.assertEqual(got, want)
+self.assertLess(peak, 64 * 1024)
+```
+
+This cannot be implemented as specified. It is not a matter of a cleverer
+implementation; exact selection from a stream has a memory lower bound.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The median is the n/2-th order statistic. On a single-pass stream of arbitrary
+values, an exact selection algorithm must effectively retain enough information
+to distinguish inputs whose medians differ. For n values drawn from an
+unbounded domain, that requires Ω(n) space (the classic Munro–Paterson
+selection lower bound; the same holds for random-order streams when exactness
+is required). With 200,000 values, 64 KB holds only ~8,192 8-byte floats, so no
+exact algorithm can stay under the limit.
+
+Intuitively: the median can be an element that has not been seen yet, and its
+rank depends on values that may arrive at any point. Constant-memory summaries
+(counters, running moments, fixed-size sketches) cannot recover an exact order
+statistic. To emit the exact median you must either keep the data (memory
+proportional to n, what `sorted(values)` does) or make multiple passes over
+stored data.
+
+This is the same trade-off the current implementation makes: modifying a
+200,000-item feed uses ~7.2 MB. The test observes 7,221,928 bytes peak; the
+even-count value is now correct, but the memory assertion still fails.
+
+## Workarounds considered and rejected
+
+The request explicitly says not to work around the impossibility, so none of
+these were implemented:
+
+- **Spill to disk / external sort.** Would keep `tracemalloc` low but is not
+  O(1) memory in any meaningful sense and would add I/O and latency to the
+  ingest path.
+- **Reaching into the generator** (re-running it, copying its state, or
+  introspecting `feed`). Specific to the test, not valid for real feeds, which
+  are one-shot and may be non-replayable.
+- **Approximate median** (P², t-digest, reservoir sampling). Genuinely O(1)
+  memory but not exact, so it fails the equality assertion and changes
+  `median()`'s contract.
+- **Bounded-domain counting.** Exact and streaming, but only when values come
+  from a small known domain; the feeds are arbitrary floats.
+
+## What is still needed (requires a spec/API decision)
+
+Pick one and update the requirement and its test accordingly:
+
+1. **Allow approximation:** change `median()` (or add a separate
+   `approx_median()`) to use a constant-memory sketch and assert accuracy
+   within an explicit error bound instead of exact equality.
+2. **Allow O(n) memory:** keep the exact implementation, fix the even-count
+   bug (done), and drop or raise the 64 KB assertion; process feeds in
+   bounded-size chunks at the worker level instead.
+3. **Allow a bounded domain:** if latency values can be bucketed to a fixed
+   resolution, replace the sort with a fixed-size histogram and assert
+   exactness at that resolution.
+4. **Allow external storage:** if disk spill is acceptable, state that
+   explicitly and size the assertion/temporary storage accordingly.
+
+## Completed in the meantime
+
+The independent correctness bug is fixed in `stats_stream.py`: for an even
+number of values, `median()` now returns the mean of the two middle values
+instead of the upper middle one. `test_even` (`median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 2.5`)
+and the other non-memory tests pass.
